# Godot 4+ specific ignores
.godot/
/android/
perf_log.csv
perf_log.csv.import
perf_log.*.translation

# Heavy binary assets (models, textures) -- not tracked in git, only the
# project files that reference them. See crosslight_flag_maintenance_cost
# memory / repo-size discussion: git kept rejecting pushes over GitHub's
# 2GB-per-pack limit once these were included in commit history.
# shaders/ is source code, not a binary asset, so it's the one exception.
assets/*
!assets/shaders/

# Exported builds -- same problem as assets/* above, just from `godot
# --export` instead of downloaded models. A macOS export bundle once landed
# in a commit here at 2.7GB (Crosslight.pck alone) and was never meant to
# be tracked -- builds are a local artifact, not project source.
CrossLight.app/
*.app/
*.pck
Crosslight.exe
crosslight_build/

# Per-branch editing workflow: rooms are saved as binary .scn files; the
# user periodically resaves a .tscn (text) twin of whichever scene needs
# Claude to read/grep it, since binary .scn isn't text-readable. These
# copies can turn up as loose files anywhere in the tree (not just under
# rooms/tscn/ -- rooms/scn/kitchen/kitchen.tscn is one example), so this is
# an extension-based rule rather than a folder-based one. Files already
# tracked before this rule was added (ui/main_menu.tscn, player/player.tscn,
# etc. -- genuinely .tscn-native content, not debug copies) are unaffected;
# gitignore only keeps NEW untracked .tscn files from being picked up by
# `git add`. Local only.
*.tscn

# Baked occlusion-culling data (Project > Generate Occlusion Culling) --
# regenerated from room geometry, not authored content, and rooms/
# estate_exterior.occ alone hit 133MB, over GitHub's 100MB single-file
# limit and part of why a push kept failing. Local only; rebake after
# pulling instead of expecting it in the repo.
*.occ
